// Sign development builds with your own certificate.
//
// Ad hoc builds (the default) get a new code identity on every rebuild, so macOS forgets that you allowed
// myData to read Contacts and Calendars and asks again. A build signed with an Apple Development certificate
// keeps one identity (your team and kiwi.mydata.mydata), so you allow it once.
//
// 1. Xcode > Settings > Accounts: add your Apple Account (a free one is enough). Select the team
//    "<Your Name> (Personal Team)", click Manage Certificates, then + > Apple Development.
// 2. Find your team ID (10 characters):
//      security find-certificate -c "Apple Development" -p | openssl x509 -noout -subject
//    It is the OU= value.
// 3. Copy this file to Signing.local.xcconfig in the same folder (git ignores it) and fill in the ID.
// 4. Rebuild: flutter build macos --debug (or flutter run -d macos). The first codesign may ask for your
//    login keychain password; choose Always Allow.
// 5. Open myData once and allow Contacts and Calendars again. From then on rebuilds keep the permission.
//
// Releasing to other people needs a Developer ID Application certificate, the hardened runtime and
// notarisation, which need the paid Apple Developer Program. A free team cannot do that.

DEVELOPMENT_TEAM = ABCDE12345
CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = Apple Development
